  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nGuidance has not been accurate with afternoon dewpoints, so did \nlower them with this forecast package. RH percentages in the 20s and \n30s will be observed across much of the area this afternoon and \nSaturday afternoon. Despite low RH percentages, winds are \nexpected to remain light which will minimize any fire weather \nrisks. \n\nDry and pleasant conditions thanks to a high pressure system will be \nthe main story in the short-term forecast period. Clear skies this \nevening allowing low temperatures to drop into the 30s for most. \nTemperatures will rebound quite nicely Saturday afternoon, with \nhighs in the 50s for our mountain locations and 60s for the \nlowlands. Some mid-to-high level clouds begin to filter into the \nregion Saturday as a low pressure system begins to take shape off \nthe southeast coast. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe afore mentioned low pressure system forming off the \nsoutheast coast will begin to march up the eastern seaboard on \nSunday. Model guidance has come to a consensus that this low \nwill track close enough to provide some precipitation to the far\neastern portion of the forecast area. Rainfall amounts are \nexpected to be light. Additionally, a cold front is set to cross\nthe region late Sunday into Monday, this front looks rather \nmoisture starved as the parent shortwave passes well off to the \nnorth. Cooler air arrives behind this front to begin the busy \nThanksgiving travel week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nHigh pressure moving into the northeast will provide dry and cool \nconditions until at least mid-week. Model guidance all continues to \nshow an impactful low-pressure system moving through the region in \nthe Thursday/Friday timeframe. It is to to early for specific \ndetails on what impacts to expect, but given the busy holiday travel \nit is worth mentioning there could potentially be some\ndisruptions.",
